# Scheduled batch jobs run here so they lose CPU to interactive work instead of
# competing with it on equal terms.
#
# The daily jobs are scheduled 03:10-06:15, and this laptop is not on then --
# ten consecutive boots put the earliest at 06:09 and the median around 09:30.
# Every one of those runs therefore arrives as Persistent=true catch-up, and
# catch-up ignores RandomizedDelaySec: on 2026-08-04 eight units started inside
# a 41 ms window (09:19:48.154 -> .195), one of them osm-truck-repair, which
# averages 33 minutes. That lands as a half-hour of contention on a 4-core
# machine at exactly the moment Boss logs in and starts working.
#
# Moving the schedules into waking hours would trade a login spike for a
# midday one. Capping the batch's share is the fix that does not move the
# problem around: the work still runs, it just yields.
#
# CPUWeight only -- 100 is the default every other unit gets, so 20 means the
# batch takes roughly a fifth of a contended core-share and effectively all of
# an idle one. IOWeight is deliberately absent: this user manager has only
# `cpu memory pids` delegated (cgroup.controllers), no `io`, so an IOWeight=
# here would parse fine, do nothing, and read as protection that is not there.
# MemoryHigh is throttle-not-kill, set at 4G of 15G total: enough headroom for
# the PBF and conflation jobs, low enough that a runaway reclaims instead of
# pushing the desktop into swap.

[Unit]
Description=truck-intel scheduled batch jobs (deprioritised against interactive work)
Before=slices.target

[Slice]
CPUWeight=20
MemoryHigh=4G
